## Chapter 13: Controlled Output
The training grounds of Anvil Ridge were a far cry from the high-tech, indoor simulation domes of the upper sectors.
It was a cleared five-acre plot of cracked concrete behind the main complex, littered with rusted shipping containers, piles of tires, and wooden obstacles meant to mimic the ruined streets of an urban Colony Zone.
The cold morning fog still clung to the asphalt as Renna stood in the center of the yard, her dented iron tower shield planted firmly in the ground.
Beside her stood two other freshmen she had recruited for her Joint Assessment squad: **Tobias**, a lanky, nervous kid with a cracked wooden heavy crossbow who had awakened a Tier-D *Sentry* class, and **Mina**, a quiet girl with quick, sharp eyes holding a pair of dulled iron daggers, a Tier-D *Scout*.
Kaelen stood at the edge of the squad line, his worn uniform jacket zipped up over his hidden, high-density bracers. He kept his hands tucked into his pockets, slumping his posture slightly while chewing on a cold piece of ration bread.
"Alright, listen up," Renna called out, her amber eyes scanning the three of them. "We have six days until the Joint Sector Assessment. Gideon Prep and Ironclad aren't going to treat us like classmates—they're going to use us as meat shields to draw out the high-value targets while their assault teams rack up points."
Tobias swallowed hard, adjusting the strap of his heavy crossbow. "Renna, I checked the public rosters. Ironclad’s squad is led by Leon Thorne. He’s already Level 8, and his Kinetic Breaker class lets him smash through steel barriers. How are we supposed to compete with that?"
"We don't try to out-damage them," Renna said firmly, slamming her fist against her tower shield with a heavy *CLANG*. "Our goal isn't to be heroes. Our goal is to secure a designated sector perimeter, hold the line, and gather enough scrap and target tags to pass the minimum draft threshold. I take the front with my shield. Mina provides flank warnings and picks off weak targets. Tobias covers the long range."
She turned her eyes to Kaelen.
"Kaelen, you're on trap deployment and salvage management," she instructed, her voice softening slightly. "In a live zone, monsters will try to flank my shield. You set wire traps, dump oil or scrap to slow their movement, and harvest the target tags from the beasts we kill. Keep your head down, don't try to engage in melee, and stay behind my tower shield if something breaks through."
"Got it, Renna," Kaelen muttered meekly, nodding obediently. "Stay behind the shield, set the wire."
"Alright," Renna raised her shield. "Let's run a tactical mock-drill. Tobias, Mina—advance on the container layout. Kaelen, fall back to the secondary line and set your perimeter."
For the next three hours, Kaelen engaged in a very delicate, highly calculated exercise.
He wasn't practicing how to fight. He was practicing **Controlled Output**.
To someone with **38 Strength, 40 Agility, and 40 Vitality**, moving like a weak Level 6 Salvager required immense mental discipline. Every step, every toss of a trap wire, every dodge had to look slightly clumsy, slightly strained, and completely within the expected baseline of a broke auxiliary student.
When a automated wooden practice target sprung out from behind a shipping container, Kaelen didn't unleash a **[Kinetic Overdrive]** strike or a 40-Agility blur dash.
Instead, using his **[Hive-Mind Synapse]**, he calculated the trajectory of the target, its speed, and the precise blind spot in Tobias's field of view.
Kaelen deliberately tripped over a wooden box—making it look like a complete fluke—and kicked a rusted iron pipe directly into the path of the target.
*CLACK!*
The iron pipe jammed the automated target's mechanical tracks, causing it to stumble directly into the line of sight of Tobias's crossbow.
*THWACK!*
Tobias's bolt struck the target's center core.
"Yes! Hit it!" Tobias yelled, pumping his fist in celebration. "Did you see that shot?"
"Great reaction, Tobias!" Renna shouted from the front line, holding off two other mechanical targets with her shield. "Kaelen, good job staying out of the way!"
Kaelen pulled himself up from the dirt, dusting off his worn knees and putting on a breathless, slightly embarrassed grin. "Sorry, I tripped. Glad the pipe caught it."
In reality, Kaelen’s pulse hadn't even flickered past 65 beats per minute.
Over the course of the drill, he repeated the process half a dozen times:
 * He subtly repositioned scrap metal so Renna's shield had better physical leverage against heavy impacts.
 * He threw wire traps that looked poorly placed, but intentionally funneled the mock targets straight into Mina's dagger range.
 * He made his squad look cohesive, capable, and efficient, all while standing in the back looking like a lucky, frail Salvager who was barely managing to stay alive.
As the sun began to dip below the industrial smog, ending the training session, the four of them sat on a row of overturned tires, panting and drinking water.
Renna took a long gulp from her canteen, wiping sweat from her forehead. She looked at the drill timer slate—their squad had cleared the mock-perimeter four minutes faster than the previous attempt.
"Not bad," Renna said, a genuine smile breaking across her tired face. "If we execute like this in the real assessment, we might actually clear the draft threshold without losing anyone."
Tobias laughed nervously. "Man, Kaelen, for a guy with zero combat stats, your scrap placements are weirdly lucky."
"Just... trying not to get hit," Kaelen muttered softly, taking a slow sip from the low-grade stamina tonic Silas had given him, making sure to look tired for his teammates.
Mina twirled one of her dull training daggers, her sharp eyes studying Kaelen quietly. "You're calm under pressure, Kaelen. Most Salvagers panic when a target springs out. You don't scream."
"I'm usually too scared to scream," Kaelen lied smoothly, offering a weak, self-deprecating chuckle.
Mina chuckled, shaking her head, satisfied with the answer.
Kaelen leaned his back against the rusted shipping container, feeling the cool evening breeze against his skin. Underneath his uniform, his hidden bracers sat flush and silent against his forearms.
His squad was ready. They trusted him, they felt protective of him, and they would provide the absolute perfect public cover when the gates of the Joint Assessment zone opened.
Kaelen looked up at the darkening sky, his eyes narrowing imperceptibly.
*Six days left,* he thought. *Let's see what Ironclad and Gideon Prep bring to the table.*
